(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-gi-i-ii-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Gastrointestinal Tract I & II — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 2)","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The abdomen is commonly divided into four quadrants by which two intersecting planes?","choices":["A midsagittal (vertical) plane and a transumbilical (horizontal) plane","Two oblique subcostal planes intersecting at the xiphoid","The transpyloric plane and the intertubercular (bispinous) plane","Two vertical midclavicular planes crossing the transtubercular plane"],"answer":0,"exp":"The four-quadrant scheme uses a vertical median plane and a horizontal transumbilical plane intersecting at the umbilicus, producing RUQ, LUQ, RLQ, and LLQ. — Slide 4 (abdominal planes)"},{"q":"On the cadaver-abdomen key, the cecum and most of the ascending colon are found in which region?","choices":["Right lower / right side","Left upper quadrant region","Central epigastric region","Left lower quadrant area"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cadaver key places the cecum and ascending colon on the right side of the abdomen (right lower to right flank), framing the small intestine. — Slide 6 (Key to Cadaver Abdomen)"},{"q":"Which viscus is identified on the cadaver key as lying in the left upper region alongside the stomach?","choices":["The vermiform appendix","The distal sigmoid colon","The cecum and appendix","Left lobe of the liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cadaver key lists the left lobe of the liver and stomach among the upper structures, with the falciform ligament separating the liver lobes. — Slide 6 (Key to Cadaver Abdomen)"},{"q":"The peritoneum is best classified as which type of membrane?","choices":["Mucous membrane","Synovial membrane","Cutaneous membrane","Serous membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"The peritoneum is a serous membrane that lines the abdomen and pelvis, with parietal and visceral layers enclosing the peritoneal cavity. — Slide 13 (Peritoneum)"},{"q":"Which layer of peritoneum directly covers the surface of abdominal organs such as the stomach?","choices":["Visceral peritoneum","Parietal peritoneum","Transversalis fascia","Renal fascia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Visceral peritoneum invests the organs themselves; parietal peritoneum lines the abdominal/pelvic walls. — Slide 13 (Peritoneum)"},{"q":"The greater omentum characteristically extends from which structure?","choices":["The lesser curvature of the stomach","The root of the small-bowel mesentery","The porta hepatis of the liver","The greater curvature of the stomach"],"answer":3,"exp":"The greater omentum hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach, draping over the transverse colon and small bowel. — Slide 19 (Greater Omentum)"},{"q":"The lesser omentum extends from the porta hepatis to the lesser curvature and consists of which two ligaments?","choices":["Gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments","Falciform and coronary hepatic ligaments","Hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments","Phrenicocolic and triangular ligaments"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lesser omentum comprises the hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments running from the liver to the lesser curvature and duodenal bulb. — Slide 21 (Peritoneal Reflections)"},{"q":"The root of the mesentery proper runs from the duodenojejunal flexure to which landmark, suspending the jejunum and ileum?","choices":["The right iliac fossa","The left colic flexure","The porta hepatis","The pelvic brim at the pubis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The mesentery proper's root extends from the duodenojejunal flexure to the right iliac fossa and carries vessels, lymphatics, and nerves between its layers. — Slide 22 (Mesentery)"},{"q":"A retroperitoneal organ is one that:","choices":["Lies behind the peritoneum with peritoneum covering only its anterior surface","Is completely surrounded by visceral peritoneum and suspended by a mesentery","Floats freely within the peritoneal cavity, unattached to the posterior wall","Is enclosed entirely within the lesser sac, behind the stomach"],"answer":0,"exp":"Retroperitoneal organs (e.g., pancreas, ascending/descending colon, duodenum parts) lie posterior to the peritoneum, which covers only their anterior aspect. — Slides 66, 93, 96, 130 (retroperitoneal organs)"},{"q":"The GI tract is described as a continuous tube from mouth to anus with an approximate length of:","choices":["About 3 feet (1 meter)","About 30 feet (9–10 meters)","About 60 feet (18 meters)","About 10 feet (3 meters)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The alimentary canal runs about 30 feet (9–10 m) from mouth to anus, with smooth muscle in the wall responsible for motility. — Slide 8 (General Structure)"},{"q":"Which of the following is an ACCESSORY digestive organ rather than part of the GI tube itself?","choices":["Esophagus","Stomach","Liver","Large intestine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Accessory organs (teeth, tongue, salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, pancreas) do not form the tube but aid digestion; the esophagus, stomach, and intestines form the tube. — Slide 9 (Accessory digestive organs)"},{"q":"The enteric nervous system provides the intrinsic innervation of the gut and is distributed in how many intramuscular plexuses running the length of the gut?","choices":["One","Four","Three","Two"],"answer":3,"exp":"The enteric nervous system is distributed in two intramuscular plexuses that extend along the gut and coordinate smooth muscle activity; the tract is primarily parasympathetically innervated. — Slide 12 (Special Features)"},{"q":"Longitudinal folds in the stomach that disappear as the tract fills are called:","choices":["Plicae circulares","Villi","Haustra","Rugae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Rugae are temporary longitudinal folds that flatten as the stomach fills; plicae circulares are permanent transverse folds of the small intestine. — Slide 12 (Folding); Slide 63 (Plicae)"},{"q":"The mucosal lining of the esophagus is composed of which epithelium?","choices":["Simple columnar absorptive epithelium","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Transitional (urothelial) epithelium","Simple cuboidal glandular epithelium"],"answer":1,"exp":"The esophageal mucosa is nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, suited to abrasion from passing boluses. — Slide 42 (Esophagus histology)"},{"q":"Which sequence lists stomach regions correctly from the esophageal inlet toward the duodenum?","choices":["Pylorus → body → fundus → cardia","Body → cardia → fundus → pylorus","Fundus → cardia → pylorus → body","Cardia → fundus → body → pyloric region"],"answer":3,"exp":"Regions run cardia (inlet), fundus (most superior dome), body, then the pyloric region (antrum, canal, sphincter) toward the duodenum. — Slide 54 (The Stomach)"},{"q":"The most superior region of the stomach, which sits under the left dome of the diaphragm, is the:","choices":["Cardia","Antrum","Fundus","Pyloric canal"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fundus is the most superior region of the stomach. — Slide 54 (The Stomach)"},{"q":"Regarding its peritoneal relationship, the stomach is:","choices":["Retroperitoneal","Intraperitoneal","Secondarily retroperitoneal","Subperitoneal"],"answer":1,"exp":"The stomach is an intraperitoneal organ; the slide on the stomach interior explicitly notes 'Intraperitoneal.' — Slide 58 (Stomach Interior)"},{"q":"The two curvatures of the stomach are the:","choices":["Anterior and posterior curvatures","Greater and lesser curvatures","Superior and inferior curvatures","Medial and lateral curvatures"],"answer":1,"exp":"The stomach has a greater curvature (left/inferior, giving rise to the greater omentum) and a lesser curvature (right/superior, attached to the lesser omentum). — Slide 54 (Curvatures)"},{"q":"Mucosal specializations of the stomach interior include rugae, a gastric canal, and:","choices":["Villi and microvilli lining the mucosa","Taeniae coli bands of longitudinal muscle","Peyer's patches of lymphoid tissue","Gastric pits leading into gastric glands"],"answer":3,"exp":"The stomach lining shows rugae, a gastric canal, and gastric pits that open into gastric glands; the stomach is intraperitoneal. — Slide 58 (Stomach Interior)"},{"q":"Which best describes the peritoneal status and rib relationship of the spleen?","choices":["Retroperitoneal, anterior to ribs 5–7","Intraperitoneal, anterior to ribs 1–3","Retroperitoneal, posterior to ribs 11–12","Intraperitoneal, anterior to ribs 9–11"],"answer":3,"exp":"The spleen is intraperitoneal and lies anterior to ribs 9–11 in the left hypochondrium. — Slide 136 (The Spleen)"},{"q":"The spleen filters blood; its two functional pulps are:","choices":["Cortex (filtration) and medulla (storage)","White pulp (lymphatic) and red pulp (filters blood)","Alpha pulp (arterial) and beta pulp (venous)","Cortical pulp (outer) and hilar pulp (inner)"],"answer":1,"exp":"White pulp is lymphatic/hematopoietic tissue; red pulp filters blood. The spleen is supplied by the splenic artery (a branch of the celiac trunk). — Slide 136 (The Spleen)"},{"q":"The arterial supply to the spleen is the splenic artery, which is a branch of the:","choices":["Superior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk","Inferior mesenteric artery","Left renal artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The splenic artery is one of the three branches of the celiac trunk and courses along the superior margin of the pancreas to the splenic hilum. — Slides 137, 139 (Splenic Artery / Celiac Trunk)"},{"q":"Which lists the three parts of the small intestine in correct proximal-to-distal order?","choices":["Duodenum → jejunum → ileum","Jejunum → duodenum → ileum","Ileum → jejunum → duodenum","Duodenum → ileum → jejunum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The small intestine runs duodenum (first, ~25 cm), then jejunum, then ileum, ending at the ileocecal valve. — Slide 61 (Small Intestine)"},{"q":"The duodenum is described as the shortest and widest part and is largely:","choices":["Intraperitoneal","Suspended by a long mesentery","Retroperitoneal","Located in the pelvis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The duodenum is retroperitoneal (except the first part/duodenal cap), C-shaped around the pancreatic head, and ends at the duodenojejunal flexure. — Slide 66 (Duodenum)"},{"q":"Compared with the ileum, the jejunum characteristically has:","choices":["Larger diameter, thicker walls, more plicae circulares, and greater vascularity","Thinner walls, fewer plicae circulares, and reduced vascularity throughout","Prominent Peyer's patches, thin walls, and a narrower diameter","Complete absence of plicae circulares, villi, and vascular folds"],"answer":0,"exp":"The jejunum (proximal ~40%, mostly LUQ) has a larger diameter, thicker walls, more plicae circulares, and is more vascular; the ileum (distal 60%, RLQ) has thinner walls and prominent Peyer's patches. — Slides 75, 77 (Jejunum / Ileum)"},{"q":"The terminal ileum ends by joining the large intestine at the:","choices":["Duodenojejunal flexure (ligament of Treitz)","Ileocecal (ileocecolic) junction/valve","Hepatic (right colic) flexure","Rectosigmoid junction near the pelvic brim"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ileum terminates at the ileocecal valve, which controls entry of material into the cecum and prevents reflux. — Slides 61, 77 (Ileum terminus)"},{"q":"Permanent transverse folds of the small-intestinal lining that do NOT flatten when the intestine fills are called:","choices":["Gastric rugae","Colonic haustra","Plicae circulares","Taeniae coli bands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Plicae circulares are permanent transverse folds that increase absorptive surface and persist regardless of filling. — Slide 63 (Plicae)"},{"q":"Which sequence gives the four segments of the large intestine correctly?","choices":["Cecum → rectum → colon → anal canal","Colon → cecum → anal canal → rectum","Rectum → colon → cecum → anal canal","Cecum → colon → rectum → anal canal"],"answer":3,"exp":"The large intestine comprises the cecum, colon, rectum, and anal canal, framing the small intestine. — Slide 81 (Large Intestine)"},{"q":"Which portions of the colon are RETROPERITONEAL?","choices":["Transverse and sigmoid colon","Cecum and vermiform appendix","Ascending and descending colon","Rectum and distal sigmoid colon"],"answer":2,"exp":"The ascending and descending colon are retroperitoneal, whereas the transverse and sigmoid colon are intraperitoneal (with mesenteries). — Slides 93, 96 vs 94, 98 (colon segments)"},{"q":"The three longitudinal muscle bands of the colon, absent in the rectum and appendix, are the:","choices":["Plicae circulares","Haustra","Taeniae coli","Omental appendices"],"answer":2,"exp":"Three taeniae coli run the colon's length; their tone forms the haustra (sacculations). They are absent in the rectum and appendix. — Slide 91 (Large Intestine specializations)"},{"q":"Which statement about the appendix is correct per the lecture?","choices":["It secretes significant quantities of digestive enzymes into the cecum","It is the widest, most distensible part of the entire large intestine","It is retroperitoneal, fixed to the posterior wall, and lacks lymphoid tissue","It is a slender hollow appendage dominated by lymphoid nodules, fixed by the mesoappendix"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vermiform appendix is a narrow lymphoid organ attached to the cecum and anchored by the mesoappendix. — Slides 85, 87 (Cecum and Appendix)"},{"q":"The transverse colon differs from the ascending colon in that it is:","choices":["Retroperitoneal and fixed to the posterior abdominal wall","Completely devoid of haustra, taeniae coli, and epiploic appendages","The narrowest and most fixed colonic segment overall","The most mobile portion, intraperitoneal with a transverse mesocolon"],"answer":3,"exp":"The transverse colon is the most mobile, longest, intraperitoneal segment, suspended by the transverse mesocolon between the two colic flexures. — Slide 94 (Transverse Colon)"},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes the colonic mucosa from the small intestine?","choices":["Abundant villi, microvilli, and active enzyme production","Lack of villi, abundant goblet cells, and no enzyme production","Presence of prominent plicae circulares throughout","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium lining"],"answer":1,"exp":"The colon lacks villi, is rich in goblet cells for lubrication, absorbs <10% of GI content, and produces no digestive enzymes. — Slide 88 (Characteristics of the Colon)"},{"q":"With respect to the peritoneum, the pancreas is:","choices":["Intraperitoneal","Subperitoneal","Retroperitoneal","Suspended by the transverse mesocolon"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pancreas is a retroperitoneal organ lying at roughly T12–L3 level. — Slide 130 (The Pancreas)"},{"q":"Which lists the regions of the pancreas?","choices":["Fundus, cardia, body, antrum, and pylorus","Head, uncinate process, neck, body, and tail","Superior pole, renal hilum, and inferior pole","Cortex, medulla, and hilum of the gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pancreas has a head (with uncinate process), neck, body, and tail, plus main and accessory pancreatic ducts. — Slide 130 (The Pancreas)"},{"q":"The tail of the pancreas is anatomically related to which structure?","choices":["The hilum of the spleen","The porta hepatis of the liver","The right renal hilum","The gallbladder fundus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tail of the pancreas reaches the hilum of the spleen; the head fills the C of the duodenum and the body crosses the left kidney. — Slide 56 (Pancreas relationships)"},{"q":"The endocrine tissue of the pancreas is organized into scattered clusters called:","choices":["Pancreatic islets (islets of Langerhans)","Pancreatic acini (exocrine tissue)","Brunner's glands of the duodenum","Peyer's patches within the ileal wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pancreatic islets are the endocrine tissue (~1% of pancreatic cells) scattered among the exocrine acini. — Slide 132 (Pancreatic Islets)"},{"q":"Posterior to the pancreas, which large vessels lie behind the gland?","choices":["Portal vein and hepatic artery only","Aorta and IVC","Left and right renal arteries only","Superior and inferior mesenteric veins only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The aorta and IVC lie posterior to the pancreas; the uncinate process passes behind the SMA/SMV and anterior to the aorta. — Slide 57 (Pancreas posterior relations)"},{"q":"Which four lobes of the liver are named in the lecture?","choices":["Cranial, caudal, hilar, and marginal","Superior, inferior, medial, and lateral","Anterior, posterior, right, and left","Right, left, quadrate, and caudate"],"answer":3,"exp":"The liver has right (largest), left, quadrate, and caudate lobes. — Slide 115 (Lobes of the Liver)"},{"q":"The caudate lobe of the liver lies between which two structures?","choices":["The gallbladder and the ligamentum teres","The falciform ligament and the right lobe","The inferior vena cava and the ligamentum venosum","The porta hepatis and the fundus of the gallbladder"],"answer":2,"exp":"The caudate lobe sits between the IVC and the ligamentum venosum; the quadrate lobe lies between the gallbladder and the ligamentum teres. — Slide 115 (Lobes of the Liver)"},{"q":"The left lobe of the liver is separated from the right lobe on the diaphragmatic surface by the:","choices":["Coronary ligament","Falciform ligament","Round ligament of the ovary","Phrenicocolic ligament"],"answer":1,"exp":"The falciform ligament separates the left lobe from the right lobe and carries the ligamentum teres in its free edge. — Slide 115 (Lobes of the Liver)"},{"q":"The liver is completely covered by a fibrous capsule known as:","choices":["Gerota's fascia","Tunica albuginea","Glisson's capsule","Buck's deep fascia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Glisson's capsule completely covers the liver; peritoneum covers it incompletely (leaving the bare area). — Slide 104 (The Liver)"},{"q":"Within the hepatic portal triad, which structure carries nutrient-rich blood that requires metabolic processing?","choices":["The hepatic vein","The portal vein","The hepatic (biliary) artery","The bile duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"The portal vein delivers blood needing metabolic processing; the hepatic artery supplies ~15% of hepatic flow, and hepatic veins drain to the IVC. — Slides 108, 121 (Liver blood supply / histology)"},{"q":"The splenic vein terminates by joining which vein to help form the hepatic portal vein?","choices":["The inferior vena cava directly","The azygos vein","The left renal vein","The superior mesenteric vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"The splenic vein runs on the posterior pancreas and joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the portal vein. — Slides 62, 135 (Splenic/pancreatic venous drainage)"},{"q":"After perfusing the hepatic sinusoids, blood ultimately leaves the liver via the hepatic veins, which drain into the:","choices":["Inferior vena cava","Portal vein","Splenic vein","Superior mesenteric vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Hepatic veins drain the processed blood into the inferior vena cava for return to the right atrium. — Slide 121 (Histology of the liver)"},{"q":"The three anatomical parts of the gallbladder are the:","choices":["Head, body, and tail","Root, shaft, and apex","Cardia, fundus, and antrum","Fundus, body, and neck"],"answer":3,"exp":"The gallbladder has a fundus, body, and neck; it lies between the right and quadrate lobes of the liver. — Slide 126 (The Gall Bladder)"},{"q":"The gallbladder is positioned between which two lobes of the liver?","choices":["The caudate and left lobes","The right and quadrate lobes","The left and quadrate lobes","The caudate and right lobes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gallbladder neck lies between the right and quadrate lobes of the liver. — Slide 126 (The Gall Bladder)"},{"q":"The common bile duct passes behind the first part of the duodenum, through the pancreatic head, and terminates at the:","choices":["Duodenojejunal flexure (ligament of Treitz)","Ampulla of Vater (major duodenal papilla)","Minor duodenal papilla (of Santorini) only","Pyloric sphincter of the stomach"],"answer":1,"exp":"The common bile duct joins the main pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater, opening at the major duodenal papilla. — Slide 64 (Common Bile Duct)"},{"q":"The celiac trunk arises at the aortic hiatus and gives which three branches?","choices":["Left gastric, right gastric, and cystic arteries","Superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and renal arteries","Left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries","Splenic, renal, and gonadal arteries"],"answer":2,"exp":"The celiac trunk branches into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries, supplying foregut derivatives. — Slide 139 (Arterial Supply of the Abdomen)"},{"q":"Which unpaired branch of the abdominal aorta supplies midgut structures including the jejunum, ileum, and proximal colon?","choices":["Superior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk","Inferior mesenteric artery","Median sacral artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies midgut derivatives (small intestine and colon to the splenic-flexure region). — Slide 143 (Superior Mesenteric Artery)"},{"q":"The common hepatic artery gives off the gastroduodenal artery and continues as the proper hepatic artery, which supplies the:","choices":["Liver via right and left hepatic branches (with the cystic artery to the gallbladder)","The spleen and the tail of the pancreas, via the splenic artery","The descending colon and splenic flexure, via the inferior mesenteric artery","The sigmoid colon and upper rectum, via inferior mesenteric branches"],"answer":0,"exp":"The proper hepatic artery divides into right and left hepatic arteries; the cystic artery (to the gallbladder) typically arises from the right hepatic. The right gastric and gastroduodenal also branch from this system. — Slide 142 (Common Hepatic Artery)"},{"q":"The left gastric artery, a direct celiac branch, supplies the lesser curvature and gives off which additional branches?","choices":["Cystic branches","Renal branches","Esophageal branches","Gonadal branches"],"answer":2,"exp":"The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach and gives esophageal branches. — Slide 139 (Celiac Trunk branches)"},{"q":"The inferior mesenteric artery supplies hindgut derivatives, which include the:","choices":["Descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectum","The stomach, spleen, and pancreatic body","The duodenum and the pancreatic head/neck","The liver and the gallbladder fundus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The IMA supplies the hindgut: distal transverse/descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum. — Slides 144–145 (Inferior Mesenteric Artery)"},{"q":"The inferior vena cava is formed by the union of the:","choices":["Two common iliac veins","Two renal veins","The splenic and superior mesenteric veins","The hepatic and azygos veins"],"answer":0,"exp":"The IVC begins at the confluence of the two common iliac veins and ascends to the right atrium. — Slide 146 (Inferior Vena Cava)"},{"q":"Which of the following is a direct tributary of the inferior vena cava?","choices":["Left gastric vein","Splenic vein","Renal veins","Superior mesenteric vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Direct IVC tributaries include the renal, hepatic, right gonadal, right suprarenal, inferior phrenic, and lumbar/azygos-related veins. The splenic, SMV, and gastric veins drain via the portal system instead. — Slide 146 (IVC tributaries)"},{"q":"The RIGHT gonadal and right suprarenal veins typically drain directly into the IVC, whereas the LEFT gonadal and suprarenal veins drain into the:","choices":["Hepatic portal vein","Splenic vein trunk","Azygos vein system","Left renal vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"On the left, the gonadal and suprarenal veins drain into the left renal vein, which then joins the IVC; on the right they enter the IVC directly. — Slide 146 (IVC tributaries)"},{"q":"The hepatic veins are notable tributaries of the IVC because they:","choices":["Carry only oxygenated blood delivered by the hepatic artery, bypassing portal input","Drain the spleen directly into the systemic circulation, bypassing the liver","Deliver the liver's processed (portal + arterial) blood to the IVC just below the diaphragm","Return processed hepatic blood back into the portal vein for recirculation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hepatic veins deliver the liver's outflow to the IVC near the diaphragm, completing the portal-then-systemic return. — Slides 121, 146 (Hepatic veins / IVC)"},{"q":"Lymph from the abdominal viscera ultimately collects into a dilated sac at the origin of the thoracic duct called the:","choices":["Cysterna magna","Cisterna chyli","Ampulla of Vater","Pouch of Douglas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Abdominal lymphatic trunks converge on the cisterna chyli, the origin of the thoracic duct that carries lymph toward the venous system. — Slide 14 / IO14 (abdominal lymph drainage)"},{"q":"Abdominal lymphatic vessels characteristically travel alongside which structures?","choices":["The peripheral somatic nerves only, running independently","The ureters, which course independently in the retroperitoneum","The major arteries (e.g., celiac, SMA, IMA nodal groups)","The bile ducts exclusively, draining only the hepatobiliary tree"],"answer":2,"exp":"Visceral lymph nodes are arranged around the major arterial branches (celiac, superior and inferior mesenteric), draining the organs those arteries supply toward the cisterna chyli. — Slide 14 (abdominal lymph drainage)"},{"q":"The esophagus, which drains partly into abdominal lymphatics, also has venous drainage into both the azygos vein and the:","choices":["Portal vein","Renal vein","Splenic vein directly","Gonadal vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Esophageal venous drainage goes to both the portal vein (via left gastric) and the azygos system — a site of portosystemic anastomosis clinically relevant for varices. — Slide 41 (Esophagus venous drainage)"}]};
